          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which number on the data sheet matters most?</h3>

          <p>
            When you compare kits, read the volume solids figure on the technical data sheet instead of the coverage promise on the box. Two kits that both claim 500 square feet can leave very different dry film thicknesses behind. At the same coverage, higher solids means a thicker, tougher floor, and that single number explains much of the price gap between budget and premium systems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is the Best Coating for a Basement, Workshop or Patio?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Basements need a moisture test before any film goes down, workshops reward thick 100% solids epoxy, and patios and pool decks call for a textured one-part coating or a penetrating sealer instead of epoxy. The space decides what stresses the floor will face, and those stresses decide the chemistry.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Basement floors: what should you check first?</h3>

          <p>
            Check for moisture. Slabs below grade breathe moisture vapor, and that vapor pushes film coatings off from underneath. Measure it before you commit to any two-part system in a basement. Many basements are better off with a breathable floor paint or a penetrating sealer; our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-floor-paint/">best basement floor paint</a> covers that side of the decision, and the moisture section below explains the tests.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Workshops and hobby rooms: why go thick?</h3>

          <p>
            Dropped tools, rolling carts and solvent spills all favor a thick-build coating. A 100% solids epoxy delivers the impact and chemical resistance these floors need, and its slower cure rarely matters in a shop you can close up for a few days. Add a clear UV-stable topcoat if windows or doors let direct sun reach the floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Patios, walkways and pool decks: why not epoxy?</h3>

          <p>
            Outdoors, a film coating has to survive UV, freeze-thaw cycles and standing water. Epoxy is usually the wrong choice there because of its UV and moisture weaknesses. A one-part textured coating, or a penetrating sealer that leaves the concrete bare, is a better fit, and on any surface that gets wet, slip resistance should come first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Will a car park on it?</h3>

          <p>
            Then read the garage guides, because hot tires, road salt and dripping fluids are the hardest test a residential floor faces. In brief, a two-part epoxy kit is the minimum, and a polyaspartic or hybrid system is better if sun hits the floor through the open door or you want to park within a day or two. Our ranking of the <a href="/best-garage-floor-coating/">best garage floor coatings</a> covers flake systems and tile alternatives, and the <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">best garage floor epoxy</a> guide covers kit counts and prep for a DIY coat.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORTLIST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Basement: test moisture first. Shop: thick epoxy. Outdoors: no epoxy. Daily parking: polyaspartic or hybrid.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When in doubt, use this rule: the more sun, moisture and heat a floor sees, the worse a standard epoxy fits, and the more a UV-stable or breathable product earns its higher price.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="profile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Do You Have to Grind Concrete Before Coating It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy, usually yes: manufacturers generally specify diamond grinding, while some water-based epoxy kits accept acid etching on porous, unsealed concrete. Film coatings hold on mechanically. They flow into the tiny peaks and valleys of the surface and lock in place as they cure. A smooth, dense slab gives them nothing to grab, so surface profile, not glue strength, decides whether a coating stays down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What surface profile does a coating need?</h3>

          <p>
            The industry grades surface texture on the ICRI concrete surface profile scale, which runs from CSP 1, close to smooth, up to CSP 10, very rough. Most thin and medium coatings want roughly CSP 2 to 3, which feels about like 80 to 120 grit sandpaper under your hand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is acid etching enough?</h3>

          <p>
            Etching with citric or muriatic acid can bring some slabs to about CSP 1 or 2, which is enough for many water-based epoxy kits on porous concrete. It cannot strip sealers, curing compounds or old coatings, and its results are inconsistent on dense, power-troweled floors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does diamond grinding involve?</h3>

          <p>
            Grinding is how professionals profile floors, and it is what polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy makers generally call for. A walk-behind grinder with a dust shroud and a matched HEPA vacuum takes off the top skin of the slab, cuts through thin old coatings and sealers, and leaves an even profile. Rental shops commonly stock them, and a first-timer typically needs about half a day to grind a floor the size of a two-car garage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you grind concrete safely?</h3>

          <p>
            The fine dust from grinding contains crystalline silica, which harms the lungs. Run the grinder with its shroud connected to a vacuum, wear a properly fitted N95 or better respirator, and keep doors open for airflow. Never dry-sweep ground concrete, since that throws the dust back into the air; vacuum it instead. These precautions apply even to a single weekend job, and a basement with little airflow makes them more important, not less.
          </p>

