        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Test a Concrete Floor for Moisture Before Coating?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a plastic sheet taped to the slab for at least 16 hours, then use a calcium chloride kit or an in-slab relative humidity probe for any project worth protecting. Concrete is porous, and slabs on grade keep moving water vapor up from the soil. Film coatings block that vapor, so it builds up underneath and forces the film off as blisters, bubbles or large peeling sheets. Even the best coating fails on a wet slab, and no product quality makes up for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does the plastic sheet test work?</h3>

          <p>
            Tape a square of plastic flat to the floor, leave it for at least 16 hours, then lift it and look for condensation or darkened concrete underneath. It is a screening test: it flags obvious problems, but a marginal slab can slip past it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which tests do coating makers rely on?</h3>

          <p>
            Manufacturers and installers use more precise methods, such as calcium chloride kits that measure the moisture vapor emission rate, or relative humidity probes set into the slab. Many coatings print a maximum acceptable reading on their data sheet, so run the test that matches the number your product names.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What can you do if the slab fails?</h3>

          <p>
            There are three practical routes. Fix the source with grading, gutters, drainage and, in basements, a sump system. Use a moisture-mitigating epoxy primer designed for the reading you have, which some systems offer as a base coat. Or pick a breathable product, such as a penetrating sealer, that lets vapor pass instead of trapping it. Our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealer</a> covers related below-grade waterproofing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long should new concrete cure before coating?</h3>

          <p>
            Fresh slabs release a great deal of water as they cure, and most coatings require at least 28 days before application, often longer for thick or impermeable systems. On a newly poured basement, shop or garage floor, plan the coating for next season rather than next month, and test moisture before you mix anything.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the time of year change the result?</h3>

          <p>
            It can. Groundwater and slab moisture usually peak in spring and after long rainy spells, and they are usually lowest in late summer and early fall. A slab that passes in a dry August may read very differently in April. If you can, test during a wet stretch or test twice, so the coating is chosen for the worst conditions the floor will see rather than the best.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Apply a Two-Part Concrete Floor Coating?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Have everything ready first, check the temperature and dew point, mix only what you can spread within the pot life, cut in the edges, then roll or squeegee the field while keeping a wet edge. Application day is all about order and timing. Before the first batch is mixed, the floor should be profiled, vacuumed and dry, the edges taped, the tools laid out, the flakes measured into buckets, spiked shoes on hand and your start and exit points planned. Once resin meets hardener, the pot life clock runs and stops for nothing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What temperature and dew point do you need?</h3>

          <p>
            Most coatings name a range for both air and slab temperature, often somewhere from the 50s to the 80s Fahrenheit, with the slab at least 5 degrees above the dew point. Warmth shortens pot life sharply, and cool conditions slow the cure. Avoid coating a slab that sits in direct afternoon sun, because uneven heating can cause outgassing and bubbles, which matters on patios and on floors beside sunny doors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you mix and spread the coating?</h3>

          <p>
            Mix batches small enough to apply within the pot life, using a drill and mixing paddle at low speed for the time the instructions give. Pour the mixed material onto the floor in a ribbon instead of leaving it in the bucket, where it heats up and sets faster. Cut in along the walls first, then roll or squeegee the open floor in manageable sections, always keeping a wet edge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you broadcast decorative flakes?</h3>

          <p>
            Toss the flakes upward and let them drift down into the wet coat rather than throwing them at the floor; the scatter comes out far more even. On a full-broadcast floor, scrape and vacuum off the loose flakes once the coat has cured and before the clear topcoat goes on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What protection do you need with two-part coatings?</h3>

          <p>
            Fresh air and the right respirator. Two-part coatings, polyaspartics in particular, give off a strong odor while they go down, and ventilation is required. Keep doors and windows open and air moving, which takes extra planning in a basement with small windows, and wear the respirator and skin protection that the product's safety data sheet specifies. Read that sheet before mixing day, not during it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long before you can walk or drive on it?</h3>

          <p>
            Follow the recoat and cure times on the label. Foot traffic, light use and vehicle traffic each carry their own wait, and those waits vary widely from one chemistry to the next, as the typical figures below show.
          </p>

