        <div id="failures" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Why Do Concrete Floor Coatings Peel, Bubble or Turn Yellow?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most failures come back to bonding, moisture or sunlight, and the way a coating fails usually tells you which one. Reading the failure correctly matters, because a bonding problem and a moisture problem need very different fixes before any recoat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does it mean when the coating peels off in sheets?</h3>

          <p>
            Large sections that peel cleanly away from the concrete almost always mean the bond failed: a sealer or curing compound under the coating, a slab that was too smooth, leftover oil, or dust left behind after grinding. Turn a peeled piece over, because its underside often shows the culprit, such as a shiny residue or a layer of fine dust.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does a coating bubble or blister?</h3>

          <p>
            Small bubbles that form during application usually come from outgassing as the slab warms, rolling too fast or mixing too hard. Blisters that show up weeks or months later, especially ones that weep water when popped, point to moisture vapor rising from below. The first kind is cosmetic and sometimes fixable. The second is a moisture problem that has to be solved, and it is the reason basement slabs need testing before any film goes on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does an epoxy floor turn yellow?</h3>

          <p>
            Aromatic epoxies amber, and sometimes chalk, when sunlight reaches them, which shows most near open doors and windows. The change is cosmetic rather than structural. A UV-stable topcoat of polyaspartic or aliphatic urethane prevents it, which is why professional epoxy floors are so often finished with one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What lifts in tire-shaped patches?</h3>

          <p>
            That is hot tire pickup. Tires heat up on the road, soften and bond to the coating while the car sits, and as it pulls away the coating lifts in the shape of the tread. It strikes one-part paints and thin or under-cured coatings most often, while fully cured two-part systems over a properly profiled slab resist it far better. If a car will park on your floor, the garage coating guide linked above goes deeper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Much Does a Concrete Floor Coating Cost, DIY or Pro?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, a DIY water-based epoxy kit runs roughly $100 to $250 for a floor the size of a one-car garage, while a professional flake system typically costs around $4 to $9 per square foot. Everything between those figures depends on the chemistry, the prep and how much risk you are willing to carry yourself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a DIY coating cost?</h3>

          <p>
            Hybrid and higher-end kits land somewhere around $300 to $700 for a two-car-sized floor, and polyaspartic or 100% solids systems often cost more. On top of the kit, add a grinder and vacuum rental at roughly $100 to $250 per day, plus crack filler, a respirator, spiked shoes and tools.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a professional install cost?</h3>

          <p>
            Professional flake polyaspartic or epoxy floors typically come in around $4 to $9 per square foot, which puts a two-car garage at roughly $2,000 to $4,500 depending on region, slab condition and system. That price normally includes diamond grinding, crack repair, a base coat, flakes and a clear topcoat, along with a warranty. Our breakdown of <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/">epoxy garage floor cost</a> goes into pricing in more detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a pro bring that a kit does not?</h3>

          <p>
            Three things a DIY job rarely has: industrial grinders that leave a consistent profile, experience with fast-curing materials that leave no room for error, and warranties that cover delamination. The honest comparison is a DIY kit, a weekend and some risk against a pro install, one or two days and far less risk. For polyaspartic in particular, hiring a pro often makes sense.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When does doing it yourself make sense?</h3>

          <p>
            DIY fits best with a water-based epoxy or hybrid kit, a floor that is not a daily parking spot, such as a basement or hobby shop, and a slab that passes moisture and sealer tests. If any of those is missing, or you want a full-broadcast flake floor with a polyaspartic topcoat, get at least two professional quotes before you buy a kit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which hidden costs should you count?</h3>

          <p>
            Add them up before deciding. A DIY floor usually means two or three days without the room, a grinder rental, about a dozen consumables and a real chance of a redo if the slab surprises you. A professional crew usually finishes in one or two days and carries the risk itself. When the gap between your DIY budget and the lowest quote is small, the warranty alone can settle it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Do You Clean and Recoat a Coated Concrete Floor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sweep often, mop with a pH-neutral cleaner, wipe up harsh spills quickly, pad any point loads, and recoat the clear topcoat before traffic wears through it. A good coating is low-maintenance, but it is not no-maintenance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you clean it with?</h3>

          <p>
            Sweep or dust-mop regularly, because grit carried in on shoes and tires slowly dulls the surface. Mop with a pH-neutral cleaner or a dilute ammonia solution, as manufacturers often advise, and stay away from acidic or citrus-based cleaners and from soap-heavy products that leave a slippery film behind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which spills can damage the coating?</h3>

          <p>
            Most two-part coatings shrug off oil, antifreeze and road salt, but brake fluid, battery acid and some solvents can etch or soften them if they sit, so clean those up promptly. Where a car does come in from snowy roads, squeegee the meltwater out instead of leaving salty puddles, and set a containment mat under each car through the winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you prevent dents and burns?</h3>

          <p>
            Jack stands, kickstands, trailer tongues and heavy tool chests put point loads on the floor that can dent or scratch even tough coatings. A square of plywood or a rubber pad spreads the load and saves the finish. Welding spatter and grinding sparks burn through most coatings, so lay a welding blanket down in the shop before that kind of work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When should you recoat?</h3>

          <p>
            Once the topcoat dulls or thins in the traffic lanes, recoat before the wear reaches the base. A light scuff-sand, a thorough clean and a fresh clear topcoat bring back gloss and protection for a fraction of the cost of redoing the floor. Coated floors looked after this way can last a decade or longer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you fix a chip?</h3>

          <p>
            Keep a small repair kit on the shelf. Most manufacturers sell their resin and topcoat in small quantities, and a chip from a dropped wrench can be cleaned, lightly sanded, filled with a dab of mixed coating and sprinkled with a few flakes. Caught early, a ding stays a ding. Left open, water and salt work under the edge of the film, and the damaged patch spreads a little further every winter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes With Concrete Floor Coatings
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking the word epoxy on the label at face value</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plenty of products sold as epoxy floor paint are one-part acrylics with a little epoxy resin mixed in. They are decent paints, not true coatings, and they will not take daily vehicle traffic the way a cross-linked system does. Look for a separate hardener, often labeled Part B, before assuming you have a two-part product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Etching a slab the system says to grind</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy systems are usually specified over a diamond-ground surface. Acid etch leaves an uneven profile on dense concrete and cannot remove sealers, so substituting it for grinding is one of the most common reasons a premium coating peels in its first year or two. Rent the grinder instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing the entire kit in one go</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mixed coating keeps reacting in the bucket, and the heat it makes speeds up the cure. Mix more than you can spread inside the pot life and you may be left holding a bucket of hardening material halfway across the floor. Size each batch to what you can apply in the stated working time, then pour it out promptly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a basement slab without a moisture test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moisture vapor can push almost any film coating off, sometimes within weeks, and below-grade slabs often carry it. Spending hundreds of dollars on a premium system without testing is the costliest gamble in floor coating. Do at least a plastic sheet test, and for a valuable project add a calcium chloride or RH test before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving sunlit epoxy without a UV topcoat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard epoxies yellow in sunlight, so the strip of floor nearest an open door or a sunny window usually discolors first. A UV-stable clear topcoat of polyaspartic or aliphatic urethane prevents that and adds abrasion resistance as well. Skipping it saves a little money today and leaves you with an uneven, ambered floor later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which concrete floor coating is the most durable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For residential floors, polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy systems finished with a polyaspartic or urethane clear coat are the most durable options. Polyaspartic leads on UV stability and fast cure, while thick epoxy leads on impact resistance. Prep matters as much as product, especially diamond grinding and moisture testing, so a well-installed epoxy can outlast a badly installed polyaspartic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Polyaspartic or epoxy: which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Polyaspartic cures faster, resists UV yellowing and usually handles abrasion and hot tires better, which is why professional installers favor it. Epoxy costs less, is easier for a DIYer to apply and builds thick films that resist impact. Many professional floors combine the two: an epoxy base coat for build and bonding under a polyaspartic topcoat for UV and wear resistance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does concrete have to be ground before it is coated?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For polyaspartic and high-solids epoxy, manufacturers generally call for diamond grinding to create an even profile and strip contaminants. Some water-based epoxy kits accept acid etching on porous, unsealed slabs. If the floor carries an old sealer or coating, or is very smooth and dense, grinding is the dependable way to prepare it, whatever product you plan to use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What lifespan should I expect from a floor coating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A water-based DIY epoxy kit often lasts about three to six years in a residential garage. Professionally installed polyaspartic or thick epoxy floors with a clear topcoat commonly last ten years or more. Prep, moisture, traffic, sunlight and maintenance decide where a floor lands in that range, and recoating the topcoat as it wears stretches its life considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I coat a basement floor with epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but only if the slab passes a moisture test. Basement slabs often push moisture vapor upward, which can make epoxy blister and peel. Check first with a plastic sheet or a calcium chloride kit. If the reading is high, use a moisture-mitigating primer system, a breathable floor paint or a penetrating sealer instead of a standard epoxy coating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes hot tire pickup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hot tires soften and bond to the surface of a coating while the car sits, then pull it up as they roll away. It happens most with one-part paints, thin coatings and coatings that have not fully cured. Thick two-part coatings over well-prepared concrete resist it, and so does waiting out the full vehicle cure time before parking on a new floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I coat my garage floor or just seal it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose a coating if you want a decorative, easy-to-clean surface that hides stains and brightens the room. Choose a penetrating sealer if you want protection from water, salt and freeze-thaw with no film to peel or maintain. In cold climates with heavy road salt, the sealer is the low-risk route; for a showroom finish, go with a coating.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

